<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tilokarat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1409">1409</a>–<a href="/years/1487">1487</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Tilokaraj</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tilokarat was king of Lan Na, in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, from 1441 to 1487, and its most powerful ruler. He waged long wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, extended Lan Na's power over Nan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrae">Phrae</a>, and was a great patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>. In 1477 he sponsored a council of monks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-chet-yot">Wat Chet Yot</a> in Chiang Mai to revise the Pali scriptures, counted in Thai tradition as the Eighth Buddhist Council.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tilokarat.jpg" alt="Tilokarat" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Security Thainam · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tilokaraj.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
